**Abstract class.** A class declared `abstract` that cannot be instantiated directly. It exists to be extended and to define a common contract and partial implementation for subclasses. It can contain abstract methods (declared without a body, which subclasses must implement) and concrete methods, fields, and constructors (called via `super()` by subclasses).

```java
abstract class Shape {
    String name;
    Shape(String name) { this.name = name; }
    abstract double area();            // subclasses must implement
    void describe() { System.out.println(name + " " + area()); }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double r;
    Circle(double r) { super("circle"); this.r = r; }
    double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}
```

Use it when related classes share state or behaviour but a base object alone makes no sense. A class can extend only one abstract class in Java. **Versus interface:** an interface defines a capability without instance state (it can have default and static methods since Java 8), and a class can implement many. Choose an abstract class for shared state and code; choose an interface for a contract across unrelated types. (In C++ the equivalent is a class with at least one pure virtual function, `= 0`.)

**`static`.** A static member belongs to the class rather than to any object, so it is shared by all instances.
- *Static variable*: a single copy per class, such as a counter of created objects.
- *Static method*: called as `ClassName.method()`; it cannot use `this` or access instance members directly, and cannot be overridden (it can be hidden).
- *Static block*: runs once when the class is loaded, used to initialise static state.
- *Static nested class*: does not need an instance of the outer class.

```java
class Counter { static int count = 0; Counter() { count++; } }
```

**Pitfalls.** Static mutable state is shared and not thread-safe unless synchronised, and it makes testing harder. `main` is static because the JVM calls it before any object exists. Constructors, abstract methods and a `static` method cannot be combined as `abstract static`.
